                "answer": "An agricultural bank is called a mixed bank (মিশ্র ব্যাংক) because it simultaneously performs the functions of both a commercial bank and a specialized development financial institution.Its primary specialized objective is to develop the agricultural sector by providing short, medium, and long-term credit to farmers for purchasing seeds, fertilizers, and heavy machinery. However, alongside these specialized developmental tasks, it also conducts general commercial banking operations—such as accepting public deposits, issuing checks, and facilitating fund transfers. Because it combines commercial profit motives with specialized socio-economic welfare objectives, it is classified as a mixed bank."
