                "answer": "Para 1: Core Concept\n\nThe principle of profitability dictates that a commercial bank must manage and invest its funds in a highly strategic manner to earn the maximum possible profit for its shareholders while maintaining safety.\n\n\nPara 2: Underlying Mechanism\n\nA commercial bank is essentially a profit-driven business enterprise that operates using public money. To generate income, it charges a higher interest rate on the loans it distributes compared to the lower interest rate it pays out to its depositors. This difference is known as the interest rate spread. However, the bank faces a trade-off: if it keeps all its funds idle in cash to maintain absolute safety, it earns zero profit; conversely, if it lends out all its cash to earn high returns, it risks running into a liquidity crisis. Therefore, the principle of profitability requires a bank to balance risk and liquidity, ensuring that a significant portion of its loanable funds is continuously channeled into safe, income-generating short-term securities and commercial advances."
