                        "text": "What is meant by the bank rate policy?",
                        "answer": "Para 1: Core Concept\n\nThe bank rate policy is a quantitative credit control method used by a central bank whereby it alters the official interest rate at which it rediscounts first-class bills of exchange or advances loans to scheduled commercial banks.\n\n\nPara 2: Underlying Mechanism\n\nAs the \"lender of last resort,\" the central bank influences the liquidity and credit creation capacity of commercial banks by changing this rate. If the central bank wants to control inflation and reduce the money supply in the country, it increases the bank rate, which forces commercial banks to raise their own lending rates, making loans more expensive for borrowers and lowering market demand. Conversely, during an economic recession, the central bank lowers the bank rate to encourage commercial banks to reduce lending rates, making borrowing cheaper and boosting investment and business activities."
