                        "text": "Do you agree with Mr. Talukdar's perception? Give arguments in support of your opinion.",
                        "answer": "Para 1: Decisive Opinion\n\nNo, I do not agree with Mr. Talukdar's perception. While managing credit risk is important, delaying loan disbursements out of pure fear of default directly violates the Principle of Profitability and damages the core business purpose of a commercial bank.\n\n\nPara 2: Theoretical Explanation of Credit Principles\n\nCommercial banks function on the dual pillars of liquidity and profitability. A bank is essentially a profit-driven business enterprise whose main source of revenue is the interest rate spread—the difference between the lower interest paid to depositors and the higher interest charged to borrowers. Risk is an inherent part of the banking business; a manager must employ scientific credit risk assessment techniques rather than avoiding credit operations altogether. Avoiding lending out of fear completely halts capital circulation.\n\n\nPara 3: Contextual Application\n\nIn the stem, Mr. Talukdar halts loan distribution out of a blanket fear of non-repayment. This over-cautious mindset is flawed because keeping pooled public deposits idle inside a vault earns zero interest while the bank must still pay mandatory interest to depositors. Instead of using proper credit analysis, collateral valuation, and verification of borrowers' creditworthiness to separate good projects from risky ones, he chose operational stagnation. His approach discouraged genuine entrepreneurs, locked up potential revenue streams, and proved counterproductive to the bank's development."
