                        "text": "What kind of relationship exists between the bank and its clients? Explain in the light of the stem.",
                        "answer": "Para 1: Identification\n\nThe relationship between the bank and its clients described in the stem is multi-dimensional, primarily functioning as a Debtor-Creditor Relationship, backed by the relationship of a Trustee and a Service Agent.\n\n\nPara 2: Theoretical Explanation of the Relationship\n\nUnder banking law, when a customer deposits money into a bank, the bank does not hold that exact physical cash as a mere storekeeper; instead, the bank becomes the legal owner of the money and assumes the status of a debtor who is legally bound to repay an equivalent amount upon demand. Conversely, the customer becomes the creditor. Beyond this basic legal contract, a bank acts as a trustee by safeguarding clients' valuable assets and a service agent by executing financial commands such as collecting dues, transferring funds, and paying bills on behalf of the client.\n\n\nPara 3: Contextual Application\n\nThe stem notes that Surma Bank has attracted a massive customer base because it places great importance on cultivating excellent relations with its clients. By maintaining a reliable network of domestic and international branch desks, the bank efficiently manages its debtor-creditor contracts, ensuring that clients can withdraw or deposit capital at any moment. By providing personalized care and executing retail financial tasks with high integrity, Surma Bank has transformed ordinary commercial arrangements into strong, trust-based customer relationships, which acts as the cornerstone of its corporate reputation."
