                        "answer": "Rubia received 'Government Notes' from her mother, which are fiat currencies issued directly by the sovereign government's Ministry of Finance rather than the central bank.\n\n\nIn monetary economics, paper currency is broadly classified into two categories based on the issuing authority: Government Notes and Bank Notes. Government notes are small-denomination currencies issued by the country's central government to facilitate day-to-day retail transactions. In Bangladesh, the 1-taka, 2-taka, and 5-taka notes and coins are legally defined as government currency. These notes bear the signature of the Finance Secretary and carry the official name of the government rather than the central bank's stamp, serving as limited legal tender.\n\n\nAccording to the stem, the 2-taka notes given by Rubia's mother contain the signature of the Finance Secretary and have 'Government of the People's Republic of Bangladesh' written on them. These exact visual and legal characteristics precisely identify the currency as a government note. Unlike central bank notes, these represent a direct liability of the state treasury and are injected into circulation to handle fractional retail exchanges and ensure currency divisibility in local markets."
