                        "answer": "The bill mentioned in the stem is an 'Accommodation Bill' because it was created and accepted without any underlying trade transaction or exchange of goods, purely to provide temporary financial assistance to both parties.\n\n\nIn financial markets, bills of exchange are classified based on their underlying purpose into trade bills and accommodation bills. An accommodation bill is an instrument drawn and accepted by individuals based on mutual trust and friendship solely to raise short-term funds through bank discounting. No genuine sale or purchase of merchandise occurs between the drawer and the acceptor during its creation. Instead, one party signs or accepts the bill to act as a guarantor, allowing the other (or both) to secure cash from a commercial bank by discounting it before maturity.\n\n\nAccording to the stem, Mr. Hashem and Mr. Mehedi needed emergency funds for their businesses. Instead of engaging in a real commercial trade transaction, Mr. Hashem drew a three-month bill, and Mr. Mehedi accepted it out of friendship. They then discounted this bill at a bank and divided the cash proceeds between themselves to meet their immediate capital shortages, promising to settle it at maturity. Since this instrument was fabricated entirely to generate quick liquid credit based on mutual reputation and trust rather than an actual trade invoice, it perfectly matches the definition and operational features of an accommodation bill."
