                        "answer": "A bill of exchange is an unconditional written order signed by the drawer, directing a specific person to pay a certain sum of money only to, or to the order of, a certain person or to the bearer of the instrument after a specific period.\n\n\nAccording to the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881, a bill of exchange is a three-party credit instrument widely used in trade and commerce. The parties involved are the 'Drawer' (the creditor who creates the bill), the 'Drawee' (the debtor who is ordered to pay and must accept the bill), and the 'Payee' (the person who receives the payment). It acts as tangible legal evidence of a credit transaction, allows buyers time to arrange funds, and can be discounted at a commercial bank by the seller for immediate liquidity before maturity."
