                "answer": "A cheque is called documentary evidence (প্রামাণ্য দলিল) because it serves as an indisputable, legally valid written proof of a financial transaction. When a bank processes a cheque, it leaves a permanent, traceable footprint in the statement logs of both the drawer and the payee, authenticating that a specific debt settlement or fund transfer successfully occurred.Furthermore, under commercial laws and negotiable instrument acts, a canceled or cleared cheque can be presented in a court of law to settle contractual disputes, prove payment fulfillment, or defend against false debt claims. This robust statutory value and internal banking audit trail make it a supreme documentary record in the business world."
