                        "text": "What is the purpose of crossing a cheque? Explain.",
                        "answer": "The primary purpose of crossing a cheque is to enhance financial security and minimize the risk of fraud, theft, or unauthorized encashment. By drawing two parallel transverse lines across the face of a cheque, it loses its status as an open cheque and cannot be cashed directly over the bank counter.Crossing forces the payment to be routed strictly through a formal banking channel into a designated bank account. This mechanism ensures that an indisputable and permanent electronic trail is created within the banking system, making it incredibly easy to track down the recipient of the funds if any dispute, forgery, or misappropriation occurs."
