                        "answer": "1. Comparative Safety Analysis of the Two Instruments:\n\nBetween the two cheques described in the stem, the second cheque of BDT 400,000 received from Mr. Arif is vastly safer and more secure for both the drawer (চেকদাতা) and the payee (চেকগ্রহীতা). This superior security stems from its structural configuration as a \"Crossed Cheque\" (দাগকাটা চেক).2. Safety from the Payee's (Mr. Shakil's) Perspective:\n\nFor the payee, an open cheque (like Mr. Mukul's BDT 200,000 instrument) carries high risks; if it is lost, misplaced, or stolen, any unauthorized finder can walk up to the teller counter, cash it instantly, and disappear. However, when Mr. Shakil presented Mr. Arif's BDT 400,000 cheque, the bank refused direct cash payment and instructed him to deposit it into a bank account. This restriction ensures that even if the paper instrument is lost, the money remains fully secure because it cannot be liquidated into physical currency at the counter.3. Safety from the Drawer's (Mr. Arif's) Perspective:\n\nFor the drawer, a crossed cheque minimizes the risk of fraud and wrongful account debits. Because the bank is legally required to route a crossed cheque through the interbank clearing house network into a formal bank account, it automatically manufactures an absolute, permanent digital audit trail. If any fraudulent misappropriation is attempted, the exact identity and account records of the recipient are captured by the banking system, giving the drawer robust legal recourse.4. Conclusion:\n\nIn conclusion, while the open cheque offers rapid liquidity, it exposes both parties to severe financial vulnerabilities. The crossed nature of Mr. Arif's BDT 400,000 cheque moves the transaction entirely out of the liquid cash sphere and into a transparent, fully accountable account-to-account banking network, rendering it the most legally reliable and safe instrument for both the drawer and payee."
