                        "label": "d",
                        "text": "Analyze the reason behind the bank's return of the cheque in the stem.",
                        "answer": "1. Expiration of the Statutory Validity Period:\n\nThe bank authorities' decision to return the cheque deposited by Mr. Jayeb is completely logical, lawful, and aligned with monetary regulations. Under standard banking protocols and negotiable instrument laws in Bangladesh, a cheque holds a strict statutory validity lifespan of exactly 6 months (180 days) from the date written on its face by the drawer.2. Timeline Analysis of Presentment:\n\nAccording to the facts provided in the stem, the special crossed cheque was issued and dated February 29, 2024 (29/02/24). Consequently, the 6-month legal window during which the bank clearing infrastructure was authorized to process and honor this specific instrument closed on August 29, 2024. Presenting the document after this calendar threshold entirely alters its operational status.3. Transformation into a Stale Instrument:\n\nBecause Mr. Jayeb delayed presenting the instrument and went to his bank account to deposit it on September 2, 2024 (02/09/24), he attempted to clear it slightly over six months after its issuance. At this juncture, the instrument legally transformed into a \"Stale Cheque\" (বাসি চেক). Banks are bound by central banking rules to return stale cheques outright to prevent outdated debits and protect the account security of the drawer.4. Conclusion:\n\nIn conclusion, the bank did not return the cheque because of the special crossing or an issue with the Ramna Branch; it was rejected solely because it had legally expired due to Mr. Jayeb's delay. To recover his funds, Mr. Jayeb must now contact the original debtor to either revalidate the old leaf with an official counter-signature next to an updated date or obtain a fresh, valid special crossed cheque."
