                "id": "fin2-ch7-cq-029-b",
                "category": "Comprehension",
                "question": "What is meant by a revolving letter of credit? Explain.",
                "answer": "A revolving letter of credit (L/C) is a specialized financial arrangement that automatically reinstates or renews its credit amount after a transaction is completed or a specific period expires, without requiring the importer to apply for a new L/C each time.\n\n\nThis type of credit instrument is widely used in continuous international trade arrangements where an importer regularly purchases raw materials or inventory from the same global supplier over a long period. Instead of incurring repetitive administrative fees, executing fresh documentation, and waiting for multiple approvals, the bank sets a baseline revolving limit. Once the importer pays off a utilized shipment bill, the credit ceiling instantly resets back to its full face value, significantly reducing transactional delays, minimizing paperwork, and ensuring a seamless, uninterrupted flow of global supply lines."
