                "category": "Comprehension",
                "question": "Why is collateral security (additional collateral) collected? Explain.",
                "answer": "Collateral security (additional/secondary collateral) is collected by a bank to create an extra safety net and fully cover credit risk when the primary security is deemed insufficient to recover the loan in case of a default.\n\n\nWhen a bank advances credit, the primary security (such as moving business inventory) may depreciate, face market obsolescence, or fluctuate wildly in value. To insulate itself from potential losses, the bank demands secondary collateral security—such as immovable land deeds, fixed deposit receipts, or personal guarantees. If the primary asset fails to cover the outstanding debt during liquidation, this additional collateral ensures the bank can fully recover its principal and accrued interest, maintaining structural financial stability."
