                        "answer": "A Letter of Credit (L/C) is a formal financial instrument issued by an importer's bank that guarantees timely and full payment to the exporter, provided that all specified shipping documents are submitted in strict compliance with the terms of the agreement.\n\n\nIn international trade, buyers and sellers operate across geographic borders and separate legal jurisdictions, creating an inherent lack of mutual trust. Exporters are hesitant to manufacture and ship expensive cargo without a guarantee of payment, while importers are reluctant to pay advance cash before receiving the goods. The L/C effectively eliminates this transactional friction by substituting the buyer's private creditworthiness with the unyielding institutional standing of a commercial bank. Once the exporter presents the valid ocean transport bills to the bank, payment is legally guaranteed, facilitating secure and fluid global commerce."
