                        "label": "b",
                        "text": "What is meant by factoring and forfaiting? Explain.",
                        "answer": "Factoring and forfaiting are both specialized trade finance mechanisms used by business enterprises to convert their credit sales and accounts receivable into immediate cash liquidity before maturity.\n\n\nFactoring is a short-term financial service where a firm sells its regular accounts receivable (usually maturing within 30 to 120 days) to a financial intermediary called a 'factor' at a structured discount, and it can be done on either a 'with recourse' or 'without recourse' basis. On the other hand, forfaiting is a more comprehensive medium-to-long term international financing tool where an exporter sells untradeable receivables, such as bills of exchange or promissory notes (maturing from 6 months up to several years), to a 'forfaiter' strictly on a 100% 'without recourse' basis. This means the forfaiter absorbs all political and commercial non-payment risks permanently, protecting the exporter from any future buyer default."
