                "answer": "First Paragraph (Cognitive):\n\nMoral hazard affects insurance policies because it increases the likelihood or magnitude of a loss due to the dishonest intentions, behavioral carelessness, or fraudulent tendencies of the insured individual.\n\n\nSecond Paragraph (Analytical):\n\nWhen a person takes out an insurance policy, the psychological security of getting compensated can sometimes lead them to intentionally cause damage or neglect basic safety measures (such as ignoring a fire hazard or concealing health issues). This deceptive or negligent behavior violates the principle of utmost good faith, resulting in an artificial inflation of claims, which forces insurance companies to tighten verification protocols, raise premiums, or reject claims entirely."
