                "stem": "Mr. Kamal works as a manager in a garment factory in Savar. He has a wife and a son. He is worried about the future of his wife and child in his absence or out of fear of losing his working capacity in the future. Therefore, to secure future financial safety, he entered into an insurance contract with an insurance company on the condition of paying a monthly premium of BDT 2,000. In this case, both the insurer and the insured have an insurable interest.",
                "stemImage": "",
                "meta": "Mymensingh · 2025",
                "type": "board",
                "questions": [
                    {
                        "label": "a",
                        "text": "What is a bonus?",
                        "answer": "A bonus (বোনাস) in life insurance refers to the share of profits distributed by an insurance company to its policyholders who hold \"with-profit\" policies, which is accumulated from the company's surplus financial reserves and paid out along with the sum assured upon maturity or the death of the insured."
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "b",
                        "text": "\"Trust is the core element of an insurance contract\"—Explain.",
                        "answer": "\"Trust is the core element of an insurance contract\" because insurance agreements are legally based on the Principle of Utmost Good Faith (চূড়ান্ত সদ্বিশ্বাসের নীতি). Unlike ordinary commercial trades where buyers inspect physical goods, an insurance company cannot completely verify an applicant's internal health or precise risk details through simple observation alone.Both the insurer and the insured must rely entirely on each other's honesty and complete disclosure of material facts. The policyholder must truthfully state their age, health conditions, habits, and occupational hazards, while the company must clearly state its terms and coverage. If either party acts dishonestly, conceals vital facts, or breaks this mutual trust, the insurance contract loses its validity and becomes voidable under law."
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "c",
                        "text": "What type of insurance contract did Mr. Kamal execute with the insurance company? Explain.",
                        "answer": "The insurance contract executed by Mr. Kamal in the stem is a \"Life Insurance Policy\" (জীবন বিমাপত্র), specifically structured as an individual savings and risk protection instrument.According to insurance law and statutory regulations, a life insurance contract is an agreement where the insurer, in exchange for regular premium installments (consideration), promises to pay a specified sum assured to the policyholder upon the completion of a fixed term or to their designated beneficiaries upon the insured individual's death.In the given stem, Mr. Kamal entered into a contract requiring a periodic monthly premium of BDT 2,000 to tackle his anxieties regarding his absence (death) or losing his working capacity in the future. Because the central subject matter of the contract is a human life, aimed at building financial safety against physical risks and mortality, it perfectly satisfies the legal and operational definition of a life insurance policy."
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "d",
                        "text": "Will his insurance policy be of any use to his wife and child in Mr. Kamal's absence? Analyze in light of the stem.",
                        "answer": "1. Economic Shock Absorption for Dependents:\n\nYes, Mr. Kamal's life insurance policy will serve as an invaluable financial shield and a vital economic safety net for his wife and child in his absence, perfectly fulfilling its intended purpose of risk management.2. Instant Substitution for Lost Household Income:\n\nSince Mr. Kamal works as a factory manager, his family relies entirely on his active employment earnings for their daily survival. In his sudden absence, this primary source of household income would instantly disappear. The financial payout (sum assured) from the life insurance company will immediately bridge this economic gap, providing his dependents with the liquid capital necessary to avoid sudden poverty or financial destitution.3. Funding Long-term Upkeep and Education:\n\nThe disbursed claim money will empower his wife to securely cover immediate living expenses, clear any outstanding family liabilities, and maintain their established standard of living. More importantly, it ensures that his son's future upbringing, clothing, and higher education expenses can continue smoothly without being disrupted or halted due to financial constraints.4. Conclusion:\n\nIn conclusion, Mr. Kamal's proactive choice to set aside BDT 2,000 every month is a highly logical financial strategy. While no amount of monetary compensation can ever replace his emotional presence, the life insurance policy successfully converts his parental foresight into a reliable safety net, completely safeguarding his vulnerable wife and son from economic ruin in his absence."
                    }
                ]
