                    {
                        "label": "c",
                        "text": "Explain what type of marine insurance policy Padma Shipyard had adopted in the stem.",
                        "answer": "Identification: Padma Shipyard adopted a \"Double Insurance\" (দ্বৈত বিমা) policy framework for their vessel.\n\n\nTheoretic Context: Double insurance occurs when an insured takes out multiple insurance policies from two or more separate insurance companies covering the exact same subject matter, risk, and interest. The total sum insured across all policies typically equals or exceeds the actual monetary value of the property.\n\n\nApplication: In this scenario, Padma Shipyard insured their ship 'Rustam', valued at 30 crore Taka, concurrently with three different underwriting firms: X, Y, and Z. The combined coverage was equal to the total face value of the single vessel. Because the identical risk on a single property was spread across multiple insurers simultaneously, it fits the textbook definition of Double Insurance."
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "d",
                        "text": "Why do you think 'X' insurance company recovered the proportional compensation from the other two insurance companies? Show arguments in favor of your opinion.",
                        "answer": "Inference/Claim: 'X' insurance company recovered the proportional compensation from Y and Z by exercising its legal right under the \"Principle of Contribution\" (অংশগ্রহণের নীতি).\n\n\nTheoretic Rule (Principle of Contribution): Under the overarching principle of indemnity, an insured cannot recover more than the actual financial loss suffered. In a double insurance scenario involving property, the insured can claim the entire compensation from any one insurer; however, that specific insurer then acquires the legal right to recover a proportional share of the payout from the other co-insurers so that the total loss is distributed equitably.\n\n\nScenario Analysis: When the ship 'Rustam' suffered a total loss in a marine storm, Padma Shipyard lawfully recovered the full 30 crore Taka from 'X' company alone to speed up recovery. Since 'X', 'Y', and 'Z' shared equal responsibility for the 30 crore Taka risk, 'X' effectively paid out more than its individual liability. Therefore, under the contribution formula, 'X' kept its own 10 crore Taka share of the burden and legally reclaimed the remaining 20 crore Taka (10 crore each) from Y and Z.\n\n\nConclusion: This recovery is completely logical and necessary to uphold the rules of indemnity. It prevents the insured from collecting multiple payouts from different companies (making an illegal profit) and ensures that the financial burden of the actual disaster is fairly distributed among all the underwriting companies according to their contracted risk ratios."
                    }
