                "answer": "Concept: The \"Proximate Cause\" (Causa Proxima) principle is considered to determine if the loss was caused by an \"insured peril.\"\n\n\nExplanation: Fire insurance policies only cover losses where fire is the direct or dominant cause. If a fire starts due to an excluded peril (like war or nuclear radiation) or if the fire is a remote consequence rather than the primary cause, the insurer is not liable. Evaluating the proximate cause ensures that the insurer is only compensating for the specific risks contractually agreed upon."
