                        "answer": "Inference/Claim: Yes, the demand made by the insurance institution for the remnants of the furniture is entirely logical, lawful, and justified.\n\n\nTheoretic Rule (Principle of Subrogation): Under the \"Principle of Subrogation\" (স্থলাভিষিক্ততার নীতি), once an insurance company pays full indemnity (the total pre-determined value) to the insured for a total loss, all ownership rights, titles, and residual interests in the salvaged property (the burnt remains) automatically transfer from the insured to the insurance company.\n\n\nScenario Analysis: Papiya Begum received the full 5 lakh Taka (the agreed total value) as compensation for her total loss. By accepting this full payout, she effectively \"sold\" the property to the insurer. The burnt debris, while damaged, still holds some residual value (e.g., scrap metal or salvage value). Therefore, the insurance company, having fully compensated the loss, is legally entitled to take possession of the debris to recover a fraction of their payout.\n\n\nConclusion: Papiya Begum's refusal to hand over the remains is legally groundless. If she were allowed to keep the remnants after receiving full payment, she would be making a \"double recovery\" (profiting from the fire), which is strictly prohibited under the principle of indemnity. The insurance company acted perfectly within its rights by claiming the remnants through subrogation."
