                        "label": "d",
                        "text": "\"Only through the insurance system can the exporter get an assurance of receiving payment\"—Evaluate the validity of this statement in light of the stem.",
                        "answer": "Inference/Claim: The statement is completely accurate; \"Export Credit Insurance\" (রপ্তানি ঋণ বিমা) is the primary mechanism that provides the required assurance for payment in international trade.\n\n\nTheoretic Rule (Export Credit Insurance): Exporters face significant \"Credit Risk\"—the danger that the foreign buyer will default, go bankrupt, or that political instability will prevent fund transfers. Export Credit Insurance covers these risks.\n\n\nScenario Analysis: In the stem, imports are hampered because exporters are uncertain about receiving payments. An Export Credit Insurance policy protects the exporter against non-payment. When an exporter knows that an insurance company will indemnify them if the buyer defaults, their \"uncertainty\" is eliminated. This assurance encourages exporters to fulfill contracts, thereby removing the bottleneck in the food grain supply chain.\n\n\nConclusion: Insurance transforms a high-risk international transaction into a secure one by guaranteeing payment. It acts as the \"financial backbone\" of international trade, proving that the insurance system is indeed the most effective tool to provide the certainty needed for smooth import-export operations."
