                        "stem": "<p>The image shows a flowchart of Agricultural Sectors. Under \"Agricultural Sector,\" it branches into: Crop-1 (Field Crops) and Crop-2 (Horticultural Crops).</p>",
                        "knowledge_comprehension": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is a courtyard meeting?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>A courtyard meeting (Uthan Boithok) is an informal group discussion held at a farmer's house where local farmers and agricultural officers gather to solve specific farming problems.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Explain the necessity of agricultural education.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Agricultural education is essential for transforming traditional farming into a modern, scientific, and profitable sector. It provides farmers with the knowledge of high-yielding varieties, modern machinery, and sustainable soil management, which are crucial for ensuring national food security.<br><br><br>Furthermore, agricultural education helps in climate change adaptation and professionalizing the sector. By understanding the science behind crop cycles and pest management, farmers can reduce production costs and increase yields, ultimately improving their socio-economic status and the country's economy.</div></div>"
                        ],
                        "full_cq": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is a courtyard meeting?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>A courtyard meeting (Uthan Boithok) is an informal group discussion held at a farmer's house where local farmers and agricultural officers gather to solve specific farming problems.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Explain the necessity of agricultural education.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Agricultural education is essential for transforming traditional farming into a modern, scientific, and profitable sector. It provides farmers with the knowledge of high-yielding varieties, modern machinery, and sustainable soil management, which are crucial for ensuring national food security.<br><br><br>Furthermore, agricultural education helps in climate change adaptation and professionalizing the sector. By understanding the science behind crop cycles and pest management, farmers can reduce production costs and increase yields, ultimately improving their socio-economic status and the country's economy.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) Describe the characteristics of Crop-2 in light of the stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Crop-2 in the stem refers to Horticultural Crops. These crops include fruits, vegetables, flowers, spices, and ornamental plants, which are typically grown with intensive care on a smaller scale compared to field crops.<br><br><br>Intensive Management: One of the primary characteristics of horticultural crops is that they require individual attention. Unlike field crops, each plant often needs specific pruning, irrigation, and pest management. They are usually grown in fenced areas or kitchen gardens for better protection.<br><br><br>Perishability and Value: Most horticultural crops are highly perishable and have a high moisture content, requiring quick marketing or processing. However, they yield a much higher market price per unit of land than field crops, making them high-value crops that contribute significantly to the nutritional needs of the population.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) Between Crop-1 and Crop-2, which one is more profitable from an economic perspective? Give arguments in favor of your answer.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>From an economic perspective, Crop-2 (Horticultural Crops) is generally considered more profitable than Crop-1 (Field Crops) due to several factors related to market value and land productivity.<br><br><br>Higher Market Value: Horticultural crops like fruits, flowers, and off-season vegetables command a much higher price in the market compared to staple field crops like rice or wheat. Because they are often considered high-value commodities, even a small plot of land can generate a substantial income for a farmer throughout the year.<br><br><br>Year-round Production: While many field crops are seasonal and occupy the land for a long duration, horticultural crops allow for multiple cropping and intensive use of land. A farmer can grow various vegetables and spices in quick succession, ensuring a constant flow of cash and reducing the risk associated with a single major harvest.<br><br><br>Employment and Industry: The cultivation of horticultural crops is labor-intensive, providing more employment opportunities per acre. Additionally, these crops are the primary raw materials for the food processing and export industries. Exporting flowers or processed fruits earns significantly more foreign exchange than staple grains.<br><br><br>Conclusion: While Crop-1 is vital for basic food security (caloric needs), Crop-2 is the driver of economic prosperity for individual farmers. For a country like Bangladesh with limited land, focusing on high-value horticultural crops is the most effective way to maximize profit and improve the rural economy.</div></div>"
                        ],
