                        "chapter": "Dhaka Board &middot; 2022",
                        "question_id": "agr1_ch2_cq8",
                        "stem": "<p>In farmer Amin Ali's area, there is often a scarcity of water during the jute harvesting season. As a result, various problems arise in retting (rotting) the jute. Last week, at a farmers' meeting, the agricultural officer was talking about the importance of using modern technology in crop production. At one stage, he mentioned a modern method of jute retting. He added that this method does not depend on rainwater. Farmers can use this method at home or in the jute field as they wish and get a higher market price due to the improved quality of the fiber.</p>",
                        "knowledge_comprehension": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is Azolla?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Azolla is a genus of small, free-floating aquatic ferns that forms a symbiotic relationship with the nitrogen-fixing blue-green alga Anabaena azollae, making it an excellent organic biofertilizer.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Why is beekeeping profitable? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Beekeeping (apiculture) is highly profitable because it requires very little capital investment and land, yet yields high economic returns through the production of honey, beeswax, royal jelly, and propolis.<br><br><br>Furthermore, honeybees act as highly efficient pollinators for agricultural and horticultural crops. Their pollination activity significantly increases the yield and quality of surrounding crops, fruits, and oilseeds, thereby providing double economic benefits to farmers without requiring extra operational costs.</div></div>"
                        ],
                        "full_cq": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is Azolla?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Azolla is a genus of small, free-floating aquatic ferns that forms a symbiotic relationship with the nitrogen-fixing blue-green alga Anabaena azollae, making it an excellent organic biofertilizer.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Why is beekeeping profitable? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Beekeeping (apiculture) is highly profitable because it requires very little capital investment and land, yet yields high economic returns through the production of honey, beeswax, royal jelly, and propolis.<br><br><br>Furthermore, honeybees act as highly efficient pollinators for agricultural and horticultural crops. Their pollination activity significantly increases the yield and quality of surrounding crops, fruits, and oilseeds, thereby providing double economic benefits to farmers without requiring extra operational costs.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) Explain the jute retting method mentioned in the stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The modern jute retting method indicated in the stem is the Ribbon Retting method.<br><br><br>In this method, instead of rotting the entire whole jute plant in a large water body, the green bark or ribbon is first stripped out from the freshly harvested green stalk using a simple mechanical ribboner or by hand. The separated green ribbons are then coiled up and immersed in a small tank, plastic drum, or small ditch filled with water for retting.<br><br><br>Because only the fibrous bark is rotted rather than the bulky wooden core (stick), this method requires a very small amount of water. Urea can be added to the water to accelerate the microbial decomposition process, completing the retting process within just 10 to 12 days.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) Evaluate the importance of the jute retting method mentioned in the stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Solution to Water Scarcity: The primary significance of the ribbon retting method lies in its water efficiency. Traditional retting requires massive volumes of stagnant water bodies like rivers or ponds, which are often unavailable during droughts; ribbon retting completely resolves this crisis as it can be done in small household tanks or pits using minimal water.<br><br><br>Superior Fiber Quality: In this modern method, the wooden sticks (patkhari) do not rot along with the bark, preventing the formation of dark spots or \"shyamla color\" on the fiber. As a result, the extracted fiber is bright, uniform, free of bark-debris, and exceptionally strong, which fetches premium prices in both local and international markets.<br><br><br>Reduced Transport Cost and Time: Traditional methods involve carrying heavy, wet, full-length jute bundles to distant water bodies, costing farmers considerable labor and money. Ribbon retting allows stripping to take place directly in the field, meaning only the lightweight ribbons are transported, while the wooden sticks remain on-field to be dried cleanly out of the water.<br><br><br>Environmental and Economic Conclusion: Ribbon retting is an eco-friendly and highly productive alternative that eliminates the water pollution caused by traditional mass-retting in open public waters. By preserving fiber quality under adverse climate conditions, it directly increases the net profit margin for farmers like Amin Ali, proving to be an indispensable technology for sustainable jute farming.</div></div>"
                        ],
