                        "chapter": "Rajshahi Board &middot; 2022",
                        "question_id": "agr1_ch2_cq9",
                        "stem": "<p>Since most of the farmers in Padmashahar village are poor, they cannot provide irrigation in the Boro season with the help of deep or shallow tubewells. However, as there are sufficient canals, wetlands (beels), ditches, and streams in the area, these get filled with water during the rainy season. Following the advice of the BADC agricultural engineer, the farmers of Padmashahar village succeeded in producing crops by conserving water in reservoirs and arranging irrigation during the Boro season.</p>",
                        "knowledge_comprehension": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is the full form of SRI?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The full form of SRI is System of Rice Intensification.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Why does the SRI method have more advantages than the conventional method? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The SRI (System of Rice Intensification) method offers more advantages than the conventional method because it is a low-water, labor-optimized, and climate-smart methodology that drastically reduces input costs while maximizing yield.<br><br><br>Unlike conventional rice farming, which requires continuous flooding of fields and excessive seeds, SRI uses significantly younger, single seedlings spaced widely apart in an alternate wetting and drying pattern. This approach saves up to 40% to 50% of irrigation water, uses 80% fewer seeds, reduces methane emissions, and promotes healthier root growth, leading to higher grain output and lower production expenses for farmers.</div></div>"
                        ],
                        "full_cq": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is the full form of SRI?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The full form of SRI is System of Rice Intensification.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Why does the SRI method have more advantages than the conventional method? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The SRI (System of Rice Intensification) method offers more advantages than the conventional method because it is a low-water, labor-optimized, and climate-smart methodology that drastically reduces input costs while maximizing yield.<br><br><br>Unlike conventional rice farming, which requires continuous flooding of fields and excessive seeds, SRI uses significantly younger, single seedlings spaced widely apart in an alternate wetting and drying pattern. This approach saves up to 40% to 50% of irrigation water, uses 80% fewer seeds, reduces methane emissions, and promotes healthier root growth, leading to higher grain output and lower production expenses for farmers.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) What kind of advice did the agricultural engineer give to the farmers for conserving water in the reservoir?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The agricultural engineer advised the farmers to adopt rainwater harvesting and surface water conservation techniques using local infrastructure during the monsoon season.<br><br><br>The core of his advice was to block or construct small embankments/cross-dams across the local canals, beels, and streams to create a structured reservoir system. By storing the abundance of monsoon rainwater instead of letting it drain away unused, the farmers could create a reliable surface water reserve for the upcoming dry period.<br><br><br>Furthermore, he suggested re-excavating silted ditches and utilizing collective community management to safely store this water until the winter season, thereby avoiding reliance on expensive underground water extraction.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) Evaluate the activities undertaken by the farmers of Padmashahar village in increasing crop production.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Addressing Financial and Resource Constraints: The initiative taken by the poor farmers of Padmashahar village is a highly commendable and sustainable approach to resource-poor agriculture. Since the farmers were financially incapable of installing and operating deep or shallow tubewells due to high fuel and electricity costs, shifting focus to free, naturally available surface water effectively bypassed their financial barriers.<br><br><br>Optimal Utilization of Surface Water: By acting on the engineer’s advice, the farmers capitalized on their natural geographical advantage—the abundant canals, beels, and ditches that overflow during the monsoons. Storing this water in a central reservoir effectively converted a seasonal waste/excess into a vital dry-season asset, ensuring that the Boro rice crops received timely irrigation without depleting the groundwater table.<br><br><br>Economic and Environmental Success: This collective water-saving activity significantly lowered the cost of production for Boro cultivation, making farming highly profitable for the marginalized community. Environmentally, relying on surface water reservoirs instead of deep tubewells prevents groundwater depletion, controls arsenic contamination, and preserves local aquatic biodiversity.<br><br><br>Conclusion: In conclusion, the community-led water conservation strategy adopted by the Padmashahar village serves as an exemplary model for low-cost, sustainable agricultural management. Their action successfully transformed their economic hardship into crop production success, proving that scientific management of local surface water is an excellent alternative to expensive mechanical irrigation.</div></div>"
                        ],
