                        "chapter": "Dhaka Board &middot; 2019",
                        "question_id": "agr1_ch2_cq10",
                        "stem": "<p>Observe the image below and answer the questions:<br><br>- Figure-A shows a rice planting layout with a plant-to-plant distance of 25 cm and a row-to-row distance of 25 cm.<br><br>- Figure-B shows a rice planting layout with a plant-to-plant distance of 10 cm and a row-to-row distance of 15 cm.</p>",
                        "knowledge_comprehension": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is AWD?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>AWD stands for Alternate Wetting and Drying, which is a water-saving management technology used to irrigate lowland rice fields with less water.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Why is soil amendment necessary? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Soil amendment refers to adding external materials (like organic matter, lime, or gypsum) to soil to enhance its physical, chemical, and biological conditions.<br><br><br>It is highly necessary because continuous cultivation degrades soil structure, shifts optimal pH levels, and depletes organic matter. Amendments optimize soil aeration, correct high acidity or alkalinity, and boost nutrient-holding capacity, which provides an ideal environment for crop roots to grow effectively and maximize yield.</div></div>"
                        ],
                        "full_cq": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is AWD?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>AWD stands for Alternate Wetting and Drying, which is a water-saving management technology used to irrigate lowland rice fields with less water.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Why is soil amendment necessary? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Soil amendment refers to adding external materials (like organic matter, lime, or gypsum) to soil to enhance its physical, chemical, and biological conditions.<br><br><br>It is highly necessary because continuous cultivation degrades soil structure, shifts optimal pH levels, and depletes organic matter. Amendments optimize soil aeration, correct high acidity or alkalinity, and boost nutrient-holding capacity, which provides an ideal environment for crop roots to grow effectively and maximize yield.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) Make a comparative analysis between Figure-A and Figure-B.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Figure-A represents the System of Rice Intensification (SRI) planting method, whereas Figure-B represents the Conventional/Traditional rice planting method.<br><br><br>The primary distinction between the two configurations lies in their plant spacing and structural parameters. Figure-A employs a much wider, square pattern ($25\\text{ cm} \\times 25\\text{ cm}$), allowing each individual single seedling plenty of room to expand. Conversely, Figure-B features highly congested row-to-row ($15\\text{ cm}$) and plant-to-plant ($10\\text{ cm}$) spacing, where clumps of multiple seedlings are packed tightly together.<br><br><br>Consequently, plants in Figure-A receive optimum sunlight, aeration, and soil nutrients, leading to extensive tillering and deeper root systems. In contrast, the crowded conditions in Figure-B trigger fierce competition among the plants for light and nutrients, which increases susceptibility to pest outbreaks, weakens the stems, and ultimately lowers the yield per hill.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) \"Figure-A can be a revolutionary step for rice production\"—Analyze with logical arguments.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Identification of the Technology: Figure-A illustrates the specific spacing profile ($25\\text{ cm} \\times 25\\text{ cm}$) corresponding to the System of Rice Intensification (SRI). This modern approach acts as a revolutionary development in agricultural production because it completely challenges the age-old convention that higher seed density yields greater crop volume, instead demonstrating that optimized plant spacing maximizes biological potential.<br><br><br>Optimal Growth Dynamics: Under the $25\\text{ cm} \\times 25\\text{ cm}$ matrix, single young seedlings are transplanted, preventing root competition. Because the spacing is perfectly optimized, every hill is exposed to ample sunlight and air circulation, driving efficient photosynthesis; this results in massive tillering (often 30 to 50 tillers per hill compared to fewer than 10–15 in traditional setups) and large panicles with filled grains.<br><br><br>Resource and Cost Efficiency: Beyond structural benefits, this system dramatically lowers input costs for farmers. Seed requirements drop by up to 80% because only a single seedling is used per hill, while the non-flooded, alternate wetting and drying watering cycles associated with this layout reduce total irrigation water demands by up to 40%–50%. This lower humidity in the canopy also creates an unfavorable microclimate for destructive pests and fungal diseases, saving expenditures on chemical pesticides.<br><br><br>Economic and Environmental Conclusion: In summary, adopting the planting scheme shown in Figure-A serves as a monumental milestone for sustainable food security. It enables smallholder farmers to harvest a substantially higher crop yield while saving input capital on water, seeds, and chemicals. Therefore, the wide-spacing layout of Figure-A stands out as a truly revolutionary strategy for modern rice agriculture.</div></div>"
                        ],
