                        "chapter": "Dhaka Board &middot; 2018",
                        "question_id": "agr1_ch2_cq15",
                        "stem": "<p>The farmers of Sujalpur village have been cultivating rice using conventional methods for a long time. This year, at a farmers' meeting, the Sub-Assistant Agriculture Officer talked to them about transplanting seedlings using a new method. In this method, it is necessary to transplant a single young seedling per hill. This method is more advantageous than the conventional method. Inspired by this, some farmers cultivated rice using this method and became highly profitable. Following them, other farmers were also benefited.</p>",
                        "knowledge_comprehension": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is Trichoderma?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Trichoderma is a genus of free-living, soil-borne fungi that is widely used as an eco-friendly biocontrol agent to suppress plant pathogenic fungi and stimulate plant growth.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Why is mulching done in crop fields?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Mulching is done in crop fields primarily to conserve soil moisture by creating a physical barrier over the soil surface that restricts evaporation losses caused by solar radiation.<br><br><br>Additionally, a well-applied mulch blocks sunlight from reaching the soil surface, which effectively suppresses weed germination and growth. It also helps regulate soil temperature, prevents topsoil erosion from heavy rain impacts, and enriches soil organic matter as biodegradable mulch materials decompose over time.</div></div>"
                        ],
                        "full_cq": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is Trichoderma?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Trichoderma is a genus of free-living, soil-borne fungi that is widely used as an eco-friendly biocontrol agent to suppress plant pathogenic fungi and stimulate plant growth.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Why is mulching done in crop fields?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Mulching is done in crop fields primarily to conserve soil moisture by creating a physical barrier over the soil surface that restricts evaporation losses caused by solar radiation.<br><br><br>Additionally, a well-applied mulch blocks sunlight from reaching the soil surface, which effectively suppresses weed germination and growth. It also helps regulate soil temperature, prevents topsoil erosion from heavy rain impacts, and enriches soil organic matter as biodegradable mulch materials decompose over time.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) Explain why rice cultivation using the new method mentioned in the stem is profitable.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Rice cultivation using the new method—the System of Rice Intensification (SRI)—is highly profitable because it vastly optimizes production dynamics while sharply cutting down the farmers' overhead expenses.<br><br><br>The method dictates transplanting only a single young seedling (8–12 days old) per hill with wide spacing instead of bunching multiple older seedlings tightly together. This wide spatial framework eliminates aggressive inter-plant competition for nutrition, allowing each individual plant to capture maximum sunlight and airflow, which naturally stimulates the development of a massive, robust root system.<br><br><br>Consequently, this enhanced root architecture drives the production of a remarkably higher number of effective, grain-bearing tillers per hill (often 30–40 tillers). The increased tiller volume paired with a substantial reduction in seed costs, lower chemical fertilizer inputs, and optimized water management exponentially maximizes the net crop yield and financial profit margins for the farmers.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) Make a comparative analysis of the two methods mentioned in the stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Seed and Input Cost Dynamics: The new method (SRI) requires significantly fewer resources than the conventional system, making it highly cost-effective. SRI uses single seedlings at wide intervals, lowering seed requirements by nearly 80%, whereas the conventional method demands dense clusters of 4–5 older seedlings per hill, resulting in high seed and nursery management costs.<br><br><br>Water Management Paradigms: The two methodologies differ fundamentally in their irrigation needs. The traditional method relies on continuous flooding, which wastes massive amounts of water, increases pumping costs, and induces anaerobic soil conditions. SRI utilizes an Alternate Wetting and Drying (AWD) schedule, reducing water consumption by 40%–50% while maintaining optimal soil moisture and aeration.<br><br><br>Tiller Growth and Development: Plant growth patterns vary greatly between the two systems due to spacing differences. In conventional fields, crowded planting limits growth, producing only 10–12 weak tillers per hill due to competition for nutrients and sunlight. In the SRI setup, wide spacing allows single young seedlings to grow without constraints, producing 30–50 robust tillers with exceptionally large, fully filled grain panicles.<br><br><br>Pest Susceptibility and Economic Conclusion: In conclusion, the structural differences also impact crop health and final profitability. The dense canopy of traditional farming traps humidity, making it highly vulnerable to devastating pest outbreaks and fungal infections that require expensive chemical treatments. SRI’s open spacing keeps humidity low, reducing pest pressure and chemical costs, which results in a higher net yield and a much more profitable harvest.</div></div>"
                        ],
