                        "chapter": "Rajuk Uttara Model College, Dhaka &middot; 2026",
                        "question_id": "agr1_ch2_cq21",
                        "stem": "<p>Look at the table below showing pH values and answer the questions:<br><br><br>[Table showing pH scale from 1 to 14. <b>pH 1</b> to 5 is labeled as 'A Soil', <b>pH 6</b>, 7, and 8 is labeled as 'B Soil' with 7 marked as Neutral, and <b>pH 9</b> to 14 is labeled as 'C Soil'.]</p>",
                        "knowledge_comprehension": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is soil erosion?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Soil erosion is the process by which the topsoil layer is detached and transported from one place to another by natural agents such as water, wind, or human activities.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Explain the benefits of the SRI method.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The System of Rice Intensification (SRI) is a climate-smart, water-saving methodology for rice cultivation that drastically reduces input requirements while boosting productivity. By transplanting young, single seedlings with wider square spacing, the method allows for extensive root development and higher tillering, leading to a substantial increase in grain yield compared to traditional methods.<br><br><br>Additionally, it reduces seed requirements by up to 80% and water usage by 25–50% because the field does not require continuous flooding. The intermittent wetting and drying cycle also minimizes methane gas emissions and lowers production costs, making it both economically profitable for farmers and highly sustainable for the environment.</div></div>"
                        ],
                        "full_cq": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is soil erosion?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Soil erosion is the process by which the topsoil layer is detached and transported from one place to another by natural agents such as water, wind, or human activities.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Explain the benefits of the SRI method.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The System of Rice Intensification (SRI) is a climate-smart, water-saving methodology for rice cultivation that drastically reduces input requirements while boosting productivity. By transplanting young, single seedlings with wider square spacing, the method allows for extensive root development and higher tillering, leading to a substantial increase in grain yield compared to traditional methods.<br><br><br>Additionally, it reduces seed requirements by up to 80% and water usage by 25–50% because the field does not require continuous flooding. The intermittent wetting and drying cycle also minimizes methane gas emissions and lowers production costs, making it both economically profitable for farmers and highly sustainable for the environment.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) Describe the methods for reclamation of the soil marked 'A' in the stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>In the given stem, soil 'A' represents acidic soil because its pH range lies between 1 and 5. Reclaiming acidic soil is essential to make it suitable for optimal crop growth and to restore nutrient availability.<br><br><br>Application of Liming Materials: The primary method to neutralize soil acidity is the application of basic materials like agricultural limestone (calcium carbonate), dolomite, or slaked lime. These compounds react with the soil solution to increase the concentration of hydroxyl ions, effectively raising the pH level back toward a neutral range.<br><br><br>Organic Manure and Balanced Fertilization: Regularly adding generous amounts of well-decomposed organic matter, compost, or cow dung buffers the soil and reduces the toxic effects of aluminum and manganese. Additionally, using non-acidic fertilizers like Triple Super Phosphate (TSP) instead of highly acidifying chemical agents helps stabilize the soil structure.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) Evaluate the importance of reclaiming the soil marked 'A' and soil marked 'C' in the stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The reclamation of soil 'A' (acidic soil) and soil 'C' (alkaline soil) is of paramount importance for national food security, environmental sustainability, and optimizing agricultural output from vulnerable land resources.<br><br><br>Maximizing Nutrient Availability: In highly acidic soil 'A', essential plant nutrients like phosphorus, calcium, and magnesium become chemically locked, while toxic elements like aluminum and iron rise to harmful levels. Conversely, in alkaline soil 'C' (<b>pH 9</b>–14), micronutrients such as iron, zinc, manganese, and boron become completely unavailable to crops; reclaiming both soils ensures a balanced nutrient uptake environment.<br><br><br>Enhancing Microbial Activity and Crop Diversity: Extreme soil pH values drastically inhibit the growth and activity of beneficial soil microorganisms, including nitrogen-fixing bacteria and mycorrhizal fungi, which are vital for natural soil fertility. By adjusting the pH of both soils toward neutrality, farmers can diversify their crop selection from a few restricted tolerant varieties to a wide range of high-yielding cash crops and staples.<br><br><br>Economic and Agricultural Sustainability: Leaving these soils unamended leads to progressive land degradation, low fertilizer use efficiency, and substantial financial losses for farmers. Therefore, systematic reclamation using lime for soil 'A' and gypsum or sulfur for soil 'C' is highly critical to restore soil health, increase total arable land output, and secure sustainable farming systems.</div></div>"
                        ],
