                        "chapter": "Milestone College, Dhaka &middot; 2026",
                        "question_id": "agr1_ch2_cq22",
                        "stem": "<p>Look at the provided diagram showing a soil pH scale ranging from 0 to 14, where the region from 0 to 7 is marked as 'Ka' (ক) and the region from 7 to 14 is marked as 'Kha' (খ). Answer the questions based on the diagram.</p>",
                        "knowledge_comprehension": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is mulching?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Mulching is the agricultural practice of covering the soil surface around plants with organic materials (like straw, leaves) or synthetic sheets to conserve moisture, regulate temperature, and suppress weeds.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Explain the role of green manure in modifying soil texture.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Green manure plays an important role in modifying the physical condition and structural arrangement of soil particles, although it cannot change the basic sand, silt, and clay proportions.<br><br><br>When green manure crops are plowed back into the soil, they decompose into humus, which binds loose sand particles together in sandy soils to improve water-holding capacity, and breaks up heavy clay aggregates in clayey soils to enhance aeration and water infiltration.</div></div>"
                        ],
                        "full_cq": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is mulching?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Mulching is the agricultural practice of covering the soil surface around plants with organic materials (like straw, leaves) or synthetic sheets to conserve moisture, regulate temperature, and suppress weeds.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Explain the role of green manure in modifying soil texture.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Green manure plays an important role in modifying the physical condition and structural arrangement of soil particles, although it cannot change the basic sand, silt, and clay proportions.<br><br><br>When green manure crops are plowed back into the soil, they decompose into humus, which binds loose sand particles together in sandy soils to improve water-holding capacity, and breaks up heavy clay aggregates in clayey soils to enhance aeration and water infiltration.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) Describe the characteristics of the soil marked as 'Ka' in the stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The soil marked as 'Ka' (ক) represents acidic soil because its pH value falls within the range of 0 to less than 7 on the soil pH scale.<br><br><br>Chemical and Nutrient Properties: In this acidic range, the solubility of toxic elements like aluminum, iron, and manganese increases significantly, which can harm plant roots. Conversely, the availability of primary essential plant nutrients such as phosphorus, calcium, magnesium, and molybdenum decreases drastically, leading to widespread nutrient deficiencies.<br><br><br>Microbiological Properties: Acidic conditions strongly inhibit the biological activities of beneficial soil microorganisms, especially nitrogen-fixing bacteria like Rhizobium and Azotobacter. Consequently, the natural decomposition of organic matter slows down, which negatively affects the overall fertility status and productivity of the soil.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) Analyze how the soil marked as 'Kha' can be made suitable for crop production.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The soil marked as 'Kha' (খ) represents alkaline soil since its pH value ranges from above 7 to 14. To make this soil highly productive and suitable for a wide range of agricultural crops, specific physical, chemical, and biological management practices must be systematically applied.<br><br><br>Chemical Amendments (Gypsum and Sulfur Application): The most effective way to lower the pH of alkaline soil is by adding chemical amendments such as gypsum (calcium sulfate) or elemental sulfur. Gypsum provides calcium ions that replace excess exchangeable sodium on soil particles, which can then be leached out of the root zone through proper drainage, while sulfur converts into sulfuric acid via microbial action to neutralize the alkalinity.<br><br><br>Organic Matter Enrichment: Applying generous amounts of organic manures, such as well-decomposed cow dung, compost, and green manure, helps lower soil pH. As these organic materials decompose, they release carbon dioxide and organic acids that naturally buffer the soil, neutralize alkaline compounds, and significantly improve the soil's structure and water retention capabilities.<br><br><br>Use of Acid-Forming Fertilizers and Selection of Tolerant Crops: Farmers should utilize acid-forming chemical fertilizers like Ammonium Sulfate or Ammonium Nitrate instead of basic ones to gradually reduce alkalinity. Furthermore, growing alkali-tolerant crops such as wheat, barley, sugar beet, and specific varieties of rice during the reclamation phase ensures economic returns while the soil health is being progressively restored.</div></div>"
                        ],
