                        "label": "d",
                        "text": "Will following this type of principle always bring success for management? Give your opinion.",
                        "answer": "No, following this type of principle (flexibility) will not always guarantee success automatically unless it is balanced with stability and careful execution.<br><br>While flexibility allows a business to adapt to changing environments and avoid losses, constantly changing core policies and operational structures without a stable foundation can trigger internal chaos, inconsistent service delivery, and brand confusion among long-term clients.<br><br>The impact of relying solely on a flexible principle can be evaluated through the following points:<br><br>1. Loss of Unique Selling Proposition (USP): By shifting from a non-stop service to a multi-stoppage service, City Line successfully filled seats but simultaneously lost its core identity as an express, non-stop carrier.<br><br>2. Operational Disruption: Constant adjustments in routes, timings, or schedules can confuse drivers, maintenance crews, and counter managers, creating administrative friction.<br><br>3. Customer Dissatisfaction: Passengers who prefer direct, high-speed travel without delays might abandon the service due to the added stoppage times in Comilla and Chittagong.<br><br>4. Increased Overhead Costs: Frequently altering plans, re-advertising strategies, and setting up new operational stops incur financial costs that might eventually outpace the short-term revenue gains.<br><br>In conclusion, flexibility is highly valuable for survival in dynamic markets—as demonstrated by Mr. 'K's timely intervention—but it must be applied judiciously. For sustained long-term success, management must strike an optimal balance between maintaining policy stability and applying tactical flexibility when crisis demands it."
