                        "answer": "The enterprise faced total disaster because the vital characteristic of \"Realism\" (বাস্তবমুখিতা) or \"Accurate Planning Premises\" (পরিকল্পনার উপাদান বা পূর্বাবস্থা মূল্যায়ন) was not taken into account during formulation.<br><br>Realism means a plan must be formulated based on practical facts, market availability, and stable cost assumptions rather than imaginary assumptions or guesswork. Evaluating planning premises requires a thorough pre-assessment of internal resources and external economic realities before launching long-term execution.<br><br>According to the stem, Mr. Kajal designed his plan on paper but completely failed to scan his industrial environment realistically. He did not evaluate the market constraints regarding the steady availability of raw materials or standard labor supply thresholds. When wages and raw material prices fluctuated rapidly, his plan collapsed under financial strain, leading directly to the shutdown of his factory.<br><br>Therefore, it can be concluded that the core reason for the business failure was a complete lack of realistic foresight during the planning phase. By ignoring real-world market supply limits and cost structures, his strategy became impractical, proving that a plan detached from reality will inevitably lead an enterprise to severe operational disaster."
