                        "text": "Evaluate the justification of the activities undertaken by Mr. Sagar to fulfill the production target in light of the stem.",
                        "answer": "Core Evaluation of Mr. Sagar's Action:<br><br>The activities undertaken by Mr. Sagar—specifically, detecting the causes of the shortfall, introducing modern technology, and arranging training—are highly justified, logical, and fully aligned with the principles of \"Controlling\" and \"Strategic Human Resource Management.\"<br><br>Point 1: Scientific Deviation Identification:<br><br>Control is the final function of management that measures performance against standards. Instead of blindly blaming the workforce for the 100,000-meter production deficit, Mr. Sagar chose to scientifically investigate the root cause of the deviation, which is the hallmark of an effective leader.<br><br>Point 2: Technological and Skill Modernization:<br><br>By introducing advanced technology and providing proper training, Mr. Sagar directly tackled structural limitations. Workers cannot achieve high-efficiency standards with backdated tools; upgrading technology combined with training bridges the skill gap, reduces wastage, and systematically raises the hourly output closer to the 200 meters/hour standard.<br><br>Conclusion:<br><br>In conclusion, Mr. Sagar's actions are entirely justified because they transition the factory from a state of passive failure to active operational excellence. These corrective measures ensure that the factory will minimize future deviations, maximize productivity, and easily meet its supply commitments in the upcoming production cycles."
