                "id": "04",
                "stem": "Mr. Hasan is the Managing Director of an enterprise. Cosmetics and food items are produced in his enterprise. He has appointed separate specialists for each department. He noticed that the market for cosmetics products in the enterprise was not going well. To improve the market, he brought a change in the organizational structure, where the functional manager and the project manager work together through mutual cooperation.",
                "meta": "Rajshahi Board · 2025",
                "type": "board",
                "questions": [
                    {
                        "label": "a",
                        "text": "What is an organization structure?",
                        "answer": "An organization structure is the established framework or system that defines the hierarchy, distribution of authority, roles, and communication channels among various positions within an enterprise."
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "b",
                        "text": "\"In an ideal organization, responsibility and authority are balanced\" - Explain.",
                        "answer": "First Paragraph (Definition):<br><br>The statement implies that in a successful management framework, the amount of operational authority given to an employee must be exactly proportional to the job responsibilities assigned to them.<br><br>Second Paragraph (Explanation):<br><br>If authority exceeds responsibility, a manager may misuse their power, leading to autocracy, lack of accountability, and organizational indiscipline. Conversely, if responsibility exceeds authority, the employee will fail to execute duties effectively, as they will constantly depend on superiors for routine approvals, killing operational dynamism. Therefore, an ideal organization must maintain a strict equilibrium between authority and responsibility to maximize workforce efficiency and ensure accountability."
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "c",
                        "text": "Which type of organizational structure was followed in the initial stage at Mr. Hasan's enterprise? Explain.",
                        "answer": "First Paragraph (Knowledge):<br><br>The organizational structure followed in the initial stage at Mr. Hasan's enterprise was a Functional Organization (কার্যভিত্তিক সংগঠন).<br><br>Second Paragraph (Comprehension):<br><br>A functional organization is a structure where the entire enterprise is divided into various specialized departments based on the nature of functions (such as production, marketing, finance, and human resources), and each department is headed by a specialized expert. In this system, functional specialists hold executive authority over their designated functions across the whole organization, allowing employees to benefit from expert supervision directly.<br><br>Third Paragraph (Application):<br><br>According to the stem, Mr. Hasan's enterprise produces cosmetics and food items, and he initially appointed separate specialized experts for each distinct department. The strategy of decentralizing management based on core technical expertise and placing functions under the absolute guidance of specialists is the defining characteristic of a Functional Organization. Therefore, it is clear that Mr. Hasan's firm originally operated under a functional organizational framework."
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "d",
                        "text": "Analyze the logical justification of the organizational structure adopted later in Mr. Hasan's enterprise in light of the stem.",
                        "answer": "First Paragraph (Knowledge/Opinion):<br><br>The organizational structure adopted later by Mr. Hasan is a Matrix Organization (ম্যাট্রিক্স সংগঠন), and its implementation is highly justified given the market crisis his enterprise faced.<br><br>Second Paragraph (Comprehension):<br><br>A matrix organization is a modern, hybrid structural design that overlays a project-based management system onto a traditional functional structure. In this framework, dual lines of authority exist: employees report to both a functional manager (who handles technical and resource aspects) and a project manager (who manages project completion and market-specific targets). This structure maximizes flexibility and permits rapid responses to market dynamics.<br><br>Third Paragraph (Application):<br><br>The stem illustrates that the business hit a roadblock because its cosmetics segment was underperforming in the market. To revive sales and resolve this issue, Mr. Hasan restructured the management layout so that functional managers and project managers could work side-by-side in close coordination. This integration of horizontal project tracking with vertical functional specialization directly describes a matrix structure, aimed at addressing the stagnation caused by the previous purely rigid setup.<br><br>Fourth Paragraph (Conclusion/Arguments):<br><br>The transition to a Matrix structure is logically justified due to the following reasons:<br><br>* Targeted Market Focus: Assigning a dedicated project manager to the cosmetics line ensures undivided attention to fix the market decline.<br><br>* Efficient Resource Utilization: It allows the enterprise to share specialized personnel and technical assets dynamically between the food and cosmetics sectors without permanent divisions.<br><br>* Enhanced Collaboration: It breaks down departmental silos by forcing functional specialists and project heads to solve crises together through mutual cooperation.<br><br>* Dynamic Adaptability: It increases the company's ability to adjust quickly to competitive consumer trends in Bangladesh's cosmetics market.<br><br>Hence, Mr. Hasan’s decision to transition to a matrix organization is completely rational and strategically sound for recovering market share."
                    }
                ]
