                        "text": "Analyze the rationality of the type of motivation provided due to which Mr. Kafil did not leave the organization.",
                        "answer": "Identification of the Final Motivation Type: The intervention that stopped Mr. Kafil from leaving the company is Non-Financial Positive Motivation, specifically in the forms of Promotion (Status Upgrade) and an Optimized, Prestigious Working Environment.<br><br>Theoretical Framework and Rationale: According to behavioral models like Abraham Maslow's Need Hierarchy and Frederick Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory, once an employee's basic economic and physiological needs are fully satisfied through high salaries, money ceases to be an active motivator. At this stage, higher-order psychological needs—such as self-esteem, social status, corporate recognition, and professional dignity—become highly dominant. If management continuously pushes financial rewards but neglects these psychological milestones, highly qualified employees (like an MBA graduate) feel unfulfilled and seek alternative options where their status is properly recognized.<br><br>Analysis of Mr. Kafil's Transformation: In the stem, Mr. Kafil's financial needs were already well addressed because his salary had doubled. Yet, he was frustrated and ready to quit for the exact same salary elsewhere because his high-level esteem needs were ignored; he held the same junior post for five years and shared a single large room with multiple peers, lacking personal professional recognition. When the management smartly diagnosed this, they stopped offering money and instead granted him a promotion to 'Manager' alongside a private, well-furnished room. This structural shift instantly satisfied his ego, social standing, and self-esteem requirements, replacing his urge to quit with intense work dedication.<br><br>Conclusion: Therefore, the authorities' strategy of providing non-financial motivation was highly logical, rational, and effective. It proves that for high-ranking executive professionals, psychological recognition, increased authority, and upgraded workplace dignity are far more powerful retention tools than simple monetary packages. This targeted non-financial reward successfully preserved valuable corporate talent and significantly amplified organizational productivity."
