                        "text": "Which theory of motivation does the manager of 'Nishat' Group believe in? Explain.",
                        "answer": "Identification of the Theory: The manager of 'Nishat' Group believes in Douglas McGregor's Theory X of motivation.<br><br>Theoretical Context: Douglas McGregor formulated two distinct sets of assumptions regarding human behavior at work, known as Theory X and Theory Y. Theory X represents a traditional, pessimistic view of human nature. It assumes that average human beings inherently dislike work, avoid responsibility, prefer to be directed, lack ambition, resist organizational changes, and are security-oriented. Therefore, to get tasks executed, managers must rely on coercion, tight supervision, strict control, and negative reinforcement.<br><br>Application to the Stem: In the stem, the manager of Nishat Group explicitly holds the worldview that his employees dislike work and will shirk responsibilities if left unmonitored. He views his workforce as conventional and rigid individuals who oppose any structural changes. Consequently, instead of trusting them, he enforces round-the-clock observation and uses coercive tactics to extract performance. Since all these behavioral traits and managerial assumptions perfectly match the negative principles outlined by McGregor, it is evident that the manager operates entirely on Theory X."
