                                                                "text": "Evaluate the importance of minimum subscription capital.",
                                                                "answer": "The minimum subscription capital is the mandatory minimum amount of money specified in the prospectus that a public limited company must raise from shareholders before it can legally commence its business operations. Its primary importance lies in ensuring financial stability and protecting public investors by verifying that the company possesses adequate working capital to acquire necessary physical assets, cover preliminary expenses, and sustain initial operating liabilities without facing premature liquidation.<br><br>Furthermore, legally securing this threshold amount is a strict regulatory prerequisite set by corporate law for obtaining the Certificate of Commencement of Business from the Registrar of Joint Stock Companies and Firms (RJSC). Without this capital cushion, a firm cannot legally enter into formal commercial contracts or issue corporate debts, making it the foundational financial benchmark for building institutional credibility and operational security."
